f 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1045 "Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186087895 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b9c5f3f8-e3c6-5fce-a6ab-ec06e754f710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088763 #edb/ref 1061 464817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a093d616-8fca-5f2c-b903-97572cc54c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088764 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088764 #edb/ref 1061 464818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088764 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be2c0f33-b0f5-523b-ad39-35a041464bec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088764 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088765 #edb/ref 1061 464822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5b266de-8e8a-5d53-9d36-27e497c6919e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088766 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088766 #edb/ref 1061 464826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088766 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1063ed51-5ff2-572d-a554-5b8902444296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088766 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088767 #edb/ref 1061 464830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46d199f3-c63f-504d-9563-18ec7b6371c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088921 #edb/ref 1064 "A **vertical asymptote** is a vertical line to which a function gets very close but never touches.\n\nFor example, let's consider the rational function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79e02370d60aec1e1e7f9e21dafbb876ea1f9b9b2de2b3d2b8b6ba14ffabfe3d.png)\n\nThe two dotted lines $x=2$ and $x=-2$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. The function gets closer and closer to these lines but never touches them.\n\nBut how can we calculate the vertical asymptotes of a function without using a graph?\n\nIn general, we can find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function using the following procedure:\n\n1\\. Factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors.\n\n2\\. Set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nIn our case, the function is already factored, and there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, to find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+2) = 0\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are $x=2$ and $x=-2.$  Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ This matches up with what we saw in the graph." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088921 #edb/ref 1065 3467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088921 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f2345022-afb6-5c16-b3bb-6baf41399880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088921 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291517 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d05da919-52fc-509f-a8f8-2528fc830fcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292519 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292519 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292519 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292519 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186291517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292519 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd09b302-f2ca-548f-87d3-3d4055d98ae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-270004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292694 #edb/ref 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b59dc04-b4de-517d-b908-41d7f801e2fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292694 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2{x}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{2({x}^{3}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{2({x}^{3}-{1}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-1)=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440230 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440231 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440232 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440233 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440234 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$\n- **III.** $x=0$\n- **IV.** $x=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- **V.** $x=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402695 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186439745 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [1759218643