=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c74e25d-895a-5838-9281-315f3c190014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562431 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{x({x}^{2}-2x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}-2x+3)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+3=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3) \\\\  & =4-12 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562562 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a2df92f1-6772-5b52-800f-86871befecc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4835b16-d349-509d-bcb0-57408b9982c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186402695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5ef6de6-3274-5f4e-8c11-7d506bd00ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186292694 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
